  "answer": "### Short Answer\nThe deadline for filing an estate tax return is within one year from the decedent's death. An extension of up to 30 days may be granted by the Commissioner in meritorious cases.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provisions are found in Section 89 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that the estate tax return must be filed within one year from the decedent's death and allows for a 30-day extension.\n\n### Interpretation\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis means that executors or administrators of an estate must ensure that the estate tax return is filed within the stipulated one-year period to avoid penalties. If circumstances warrant, they can apply for a short extension, but this must be justified and is limited to an additional 30 days. Proper planning and timely filing are essential to comply with tax obligations and avoid complications.",
